&lt;p&gt;Tillamook State Forest&mdash;An unusually large illegal dump site in the Tillamook State Forest has been cleaned up thanks to the work of adults in custody (AICs) from South Fork Forest Camp (SFFC).&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;It was a big mess and a risk to the public and the environment,&rdquo; said Zach Rabe, the Oregon Department of Forestry&rsquo;s Tillamook District Operations Coordinator. &ldquo;The South Fork crew hauled off 12 dump trailers full of garbage.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When the SFFC crews work on illegal dump site cleanups it&rsquo;s typically a one- or two-day task.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;This one site took us a week,&rdquo; said Chad Powell, Institution Work Programs Coordinator at SFFC. &ldquo;It looked like someone took the entire contents of their house and put it in the woods. Even though this is dirty and kind of gross work, the AICs took pride in working fast and loading the trailer efficiently to make as few trips as possible.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While AICs wear basic protective equipment when handling materials, it is important to ensure that more dangerous substances are not present. Before the crew could begin the cleanup, law enforcement swept the camp for hazardous materials.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Safety is a top concern when we remove any illegal dump site&mdash;we just don&rsquo;t know what is in these sites,&rdquo; said Rabe. &ldquo;If any hazardous materials are found, then we must hire HAZMAT contractors who wear full personal protective equipment. Those types of cleanups are very expensive.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;None was found at this site on Fox Ridge Road off State Highway 6, so SFFC crews could do the work. Even so, Rabe estimated it would end up costing $8,000 to remove all the junk.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Illegal dumping in Oregon&rsquo;s State Forests is a growing and expensive problem.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;We are not sure why, but since 2021 we have seen a lot more illegal dumping,&rdquo; said Rabe.&amp;nbsp; &ldquo;For example, we would typically find just one or two abandoned vehicles in our district each year. Now we are seeing around 10 a year.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Those cars and large RVs are expensive and difficult to remove.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Some are burned out, and most are in remote areas, so it is hard to find towing companies willing to do the work,&rdquo; said Rabe. &ldquo;When they are able to be accessed for removal, getting them out of the forest costs around $3,700 for an RV and about $550 for a car.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Although ODF workers find some dump sites and abandoned vehicles, many are reported by concerned citizens.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;If you see a dump site or abandoned vehicle, take a photo and note the location and contact us,&rdquo; said Rabe.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The dumping problem has grown so much and is so difficult to track that ODF just went live with a new mapping system to keep better track of garbage, vehicles and vandalism.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;We just started using the new Vandalism Activity Tracking System last week,&rdquo; said Rabe.&amp;nbsp; &ldquo;It is a field map-based system that allows us to plot points and mark whether it is a garbage, vehicle or vandalism act that we need to take some action to remediate.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;With the new tool and better tracking, the Tillamook district and other ODF districts should be able to budget better for cleanup costs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;This should allow us to better understand this growing problem and hopefully find more funding, whether through our budget or by applying for grants, to fix these problems.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Back at South Fork Forest Camp, which is jointly owned and operated by the Oregon Departments of Corrections and Forestry, the AICs do much more for state forests than just cleanups.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Nearly all of our 120 or so AICs are trained in wildfire suppression&mdash;and are assigned to&amp;nbsp; 10-person crews to fight wildfires, we have workshops that supply all the wooden signs for state forests, they are a key part of the recreation program helping to maintain trails and doing building projects at campgrounds, there is a fish hatchery on site and much more,&rdquo; said Powell.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Not only does SFFC provide cost effective, skilled AICs for state forests, but it gives the AICs a great opportunity to improve their job skills.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;One of our main objectives is to help reduce recidivism by modeling pro social behavior and teach work skills that help adults in custody be productive citizens upon release,&rdquo; said Powell. &ldquo;We are doing that here every day and setting these men up for a chance at a successful future.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Go &lt;a href="https://www.oregon.gov/odf/forestbenefits/pages/rehabilitation.aspx"&gt;here&lt;/a&gt; for more information about SFFC.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

