&lt;p dir="ltr" style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Shaping the Future of School Leadership in Rural Communities&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size:11pt"&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr" style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Renewed funding ensures rural school leaders receive high-quality professional learning through UW Center for Educational Leadership.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size:11pt"&gt;We are thrilled to announce the continuation of the Aspiring Leaders program, thanks to renewed financial support from Ballmer Group. Before this expanded investment, College Spark Washington generously supported one year of programming for two initial cohorts, helping establish a strong foundation for the work. This vital five-year partnership ensures that local school leaders, particularly those serving rural communities, have access to high-quality professional learning delivered in collaboration with the University of Washington Center for Educational Leadership (UW CEL).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size:11pt"&gt;The Aspiring Leaders program is specifically designed to equip current and future school leaders with the skills and perspective needed to foster positive change within their schools. A key focus of the training is on lifting the voices of students to directly inform school strategies and create a positive, lasting impact on student learning experiences.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size:11pt"&gt;&ldquo;The quality of leadership in our schools is directly tied to the success of our students,&rdquo; said Alicia Jacob, Assistant Superintendent of Educational Services at ESD 105. &ldquo;Thanks to Ballmer Group&rsquo;s investment, we can continue to partner with the experts at UW CEL to provide tailored, practical training that addresses the unique needs of our rural schools. This work is fundamentally about empowering leaders who will, in turn, empower our students.&rdquo;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size:11pt"&gt;A unique and invaluable component of the program is the opportunity it provides for peer connection and regional collaboration. Leaders from various districts across the region can meet, observe, reflect, and grow together, creating a supportive network that extends beyond formal training sessions. This collaborative approach enhances professional reflection and accelerates the adoption of best practices.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size:11pt"&gt;The ongoing support from Ballmer Group highlights their commitment to strengthening regional educational systems and ensuring that students in all communities&mdash;especially those in rural areas&mdash;benefit from strong, visionary school leadership and feel a sense of belonging and relevance within their educational experience.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;For more information, please contact:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style="font-size:11pt"&gt; Alicia Jacobs, Assistant Superintendent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p dir="ltr"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About ESD 105:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;span style="font-size:11pt"&gt;ESD 105 serves the educational needs of schools and students in South Central Washington. Through partnerships, internal collaboration, and innovation, ESD 105 serves and supports students, families, and communities by removing barriers and transforming lives.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Salem &ndash; TK Keen has been chosen as the Oregon insurance commissioner, the Oregon Department of Consumer and Business Services (DCBS) announced today.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keen has been serving in the acting insurance commissioner role since June and was deputy insurance commissioner for five years before that. He is also the administrator of the Oregon Division of Financial Regulation (DFR), part of DCBS. Keen was appointed by DCBS Director Sean O&rsquo;Day, who was nominated by Gov. Tina Kotek to lead the agency and confirmed last month by the Oregon Senate. As director of DCBS &ndash; the state&rsquo;s largest consumer protection and business regulatory agency &ndash; O&rsquo;Day can delegate the insurance commissioner duties to another member of the DCBS leadership team.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Under Keen&rsquo;s leadership, DFR earned its five-year accreditation from the National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC) after a comprehensive review of DFR&rsquo;s financial analysis and examination programs. Accreditation ensures Oregon&rsquo;s oversight of insurance companies meets national standards, protects consumers, and provides assurance to other states that they can rely on Oregon&rsquo;s work. Oregon is home to 40 domestic insurance companies and 1,507 foreign insurance companies and has $24.5 billion in 2024 premium volume.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;TK has taken a lead role in DFR&rsquo;s accreditation with the NAIC, organizing its response to emerging issues and technologies, and driving efforts around diversity, equity, and inclusion,&rdquo; said O&rsquo;Day. &ldquo;He prioritizes consumer protection and the financial stability of our regulated institutions, and I appreciate him taking on the insurance commissioner role.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keen has played a central role in DFR&rsquo;s response to the evolving insurance risks involving wildfire. Most recently, he worked to implement Senate Bill 85 (2025) with the Oregon State Fire Marshal and Insurance Institute for Business and Home Safety on community risk reduction initiatives and Senate Bill 829 (2025), which contains a broad study of a possible reinsurance program for property markets. Also, he has led DFR efforts to steadily work toward broadening consumer disclosures and transparency as to rates charged in Oregon&rsquo;s insurance markets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keen led DFR&rsquo;s 2026 health insurance rate review during a period of significant federal policy uncertainty and court challenges that persisted until rates were finalized in October. Working with insurers, advocacy groups, and state partners, DFR completed the review while maintaining its commitment to transparency and market stability for Oregon consumers and businesses.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;The work of the insurance commissioner is vital to Oregonians, especially during a time of rising natural disaster risks and financial uncertainties,&rdquo; said Keen. &ldquo;I am proud to lead a team that is filled with caring and intelligent people who are dedicated to protecting consumers and working with the state&rsquo;s insurance and financial services industries to find ways to help Oregonians fulfill their financial goals.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keen has been with DFR since 2012. Before joining the division, he practiced law as a sole practitioner in Washington, focusing on employment law cases. During law school at Lewis and Clark College in Portland, he worked for the Oregon Department of Justice, the Hon. Elizabeth L. Perris of the U.S. Bankruptcy Court, and the Portland State University Office of General Counsel.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Keen added that consumer protection is a vital part of what DFR does. The division&rsquo;s consumer advocates are available to help people by phone at 1-888-877-4894 (toll-free) or email at &lt;a href="mailto:dfr.insurancehelp@dcbs.oregon.gov"&gt;dfr.insurancehelp@dcbs.oregon.gov&lt;/a&gt; or &lt;a href="mailto:dfr.financialserviceshelp@dcbs.oregon.gov"&gt;dfr.financialserviceshelp@dcbs.oregon.gov&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style="text-align: center;"&gt;###&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a name="_Hlk167350716"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About Oregon DFR:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt; The Division of Financial Regulation protects consumers and regulates insurance, depository institutions, trust companies, securities, and consumer financial products and services. The division is part of the Department of Consumer and Business Services, Oregon&rsquo;s largest consumer protection and business regulatory agency. Visit dfr.oregon.gov and dcbs.oregon.gov.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Salem, Ore. &ndash;&amp;nbsp;The Oregon Lottery will return more than $887 million to the state for the 2025 fiscal year. Compared to the previous year, the transfer is approximately $60 million less due largely to 2024 administrative savings that weren&rsquo;t available this year. The transferred amount includes unclaimed prizes totaling $9 million.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href="https://www.oregonlottery.org/oregon-wins/"&gt;funding directly benefits&lt;/a&gt; state parks and natural habitats, public schools, veteran services, outdoor school, and economic development. Oregon Lottery is self-funded through the sale of games, not tax dollars, and voters and the Legislature designate where the money goes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&ldquo;Playing Oregon Lottery games helps fund critical Oregon programs,&rdquo; said Oregon Lottery Director Mike Wells. &ldquo;Wins don&rsquo;t just benefit players, they also boost local retailers and contribute directly to local communities.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Oregon had 12 players who took home prizes worth $1 million or more, including four Powerball wins and four Oregon Megabucks wins. Overall, lucky lottery players earned payouts totaling nearly $15.9 billion for the year. Prizes account for about 92% of the Lottery dollars played.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sales commissions on Lottery games also support retailers, many of whom are small business owners. The 3,800 Lottery retailers across Oregon collectively earned $306 million in commissions this year.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Since 2016, Oregon Lottery has maintained the highest level of accreditation for responsible gambling from both the National Association of State and Provincial Lotteries/National Council on Problem Gambling and the World Lottery Association&rsquo;s (WLA) Responsible Gambling programs. Oregon is one of only 11 lotteries in the U.S. to reach this level by the WLA.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Lottery&rsquo;s financial year runs from July 1, 2024 to June 30, 2025. A final audited report is posted to &lt;a href="https://www.oregonlottery.org/about/how-we-operate/"&gt;Oregon Lottery&rsquo;s website&lt;/a&gt; under &ldquo;Proceeds and Financials.&rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Celebrating 40 years of selling games since April 25, 1985,&amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;Oregon Lottery has earned nearly $16.5 billion for economic development, public education, outdoor school, state parks,&nbsp;veteran&nbsp;services, and watershed enhancements. For more information on the Oregon Lottery visit&nbsp;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.oregonlottery.org/"&gt;&lt;em&gt;www.oregonlottery.org&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

